## 7. Preventing Duplicate 5m Processing

- **Case A (chart = 5m):** trivial — one chart-native confirmed bar is one 5m bar, no
  duplication risk beyond what M5–M10 already guarantee today via `barstate.isconfirmed`.
- **Case B (chart > 5m):** `request.security_lower_tf()` returns, on each chart-bar
  execution, only the intrabars newly available *within that chart bar's own dilation* — it
  does not re-return already-processed intrabars from a prior chart bar. This is the
  documented behavior of the function. The remaining risk is at the boundary of the
  currently-forming (unconfirmed) chart bar: the returned array's *last* element can
  correspond to a still-open, not-yet-closed 5-minute intrabar while the chart bar itself is
  still in progress. Community documentation and examples consistently describe
  `request.security_lower_tf()` as surfacing completed intrabars, but this specific edge —
  whether the final array element on an unconfirmed outer bar is guaranteed already-closed or
  can be the still-forming one — is exactly the kind of claim this report should not assert
  without a direct reference-manual citation, and none was surfaced in the searches performed
  for this pass. **This is the single most important item to verify directly against the
  Pine v6 reference manual before implementation**, since getting it wrong would mean
  processing an incomplete 5m bar as if it were final.
- **Case C (chart < 5m):** the timestamp-change detection in §4, gated on the outer bar's
  `barstate.isconfirmed`, inherently fires at most once per genuinely new 5m close, since the
  fetched value only changes when the underlying 5m bar actually closes.

