## 11. Performance / Resource Risks

- **`request.security()` call-count ceiling of 40 per script.** The current script already
  uses 3 for the MTF bias engine (`P9_HTF_1/2/3`). The Data Layer adds at most a small,
  fixed number more (one scalar 5m fetch for Case C, or a handful of
  `request.security_lower_tf()` calls for Case B) — comfortably within budget, but worth
  counting explicitly once implemented rather than assumed safe.
- **Intrabar retrieval caps** (100K–200K bars depending on plan tier) and the
  `calc_bars_count` parameter bound how much 5m intrabar history is available per
  `request.security_lower_tf()` call — a real ceiling on how far back a long historical
  replay on a high chart timeframe (e.g., Daily or Weekly) can go before intrabar data
  becomes unavailable, confirming the draft's own §12 concern about very high chart
  timeframes.
- **Per-chart-bar computation multiplication.** Running the (partially reimplemented) M5–M10
  stack up to six times per chart bar on a 30-minute chart (or more on higher timeframes)
  multiplies the per-bar computation cost proportionally — this is a real, not hypothetical,
  performance question for long backtests on high chart timeframes, as the draft already
  flagged, now confirmed as architecturally unavoidable given the intrabar-sequencing
  requirement (Architecture v1.0 §5).
- **Manual buffer maintenance overhead** (ring buffers for OHLC history, running ATR state)
  is lightweight compared to the pipeline logic itself and not expected to be a material
  performance concern.

