## §3. Data Acquisition — Chart Timeframe Above 5m (Case B: 15m/30m/1H+) — [NEW DATA-LAYER RESPONSIBILITY]

`request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, "5", <expression>)` is the mechanism, per the
feasibility report. It is valid for any chart timeframe strictly above "5," returns one
array element per contained 5-minute intrabar, and is TradingView's documented approach for
intrabar analysis (in preference to bare `request.security()`, which surfaces only the
last-merged intrabar per chart bar).

The expression passed to this call fetches **data only** — 5m open/high/low/close/time
(and, optionally, a native 5m ATR as a cross-check value, §9 test 1). It does not call any
M5–M9 function and performs no registry mutation, per §1.

**[OPEN VERIFICATION REQUIRED]** Whether `request.security_lower_tf()` supports a single
tuple call returning multiple synchronized arrays —
`[oArr, hArr, lArr, cArr] = request.security_lower_tf(id, "5", [open, high, low, close])` —
with index alignment guaranteed by construction, versus requiring separate calls whose
alignment must be verified empirically. Community usage suggests both patterns exist; no
authoritative confirmation was surfaced for the lower-timeframe variant specifically. Must
be checked against the reference manual at implementation time.

**Confirmed, not open:** intrabar retrieval is capped at 100K–200K bars depending on plan
tier, and `calc_bars_count` governs how much intrabar history a given call can retrieve.
Both are real constraints on long historical replay at high chart timeframes (§11).

