## §7. 5-Minute Bar Counter, Confirmation, and Deduplication — [NEW DATA-LAYER RESPONSIBILITY]

### 7.1 Bar counter

- A new counter, native to this layer, replaces `bar_index` for every M5–M10 sequencing/
  expiry use (`poi.createdAt == bar_index`, `bar_index - cand.createdAt`,
  `bar_index != inst.createdAt`, and equivalents).
- Increments by exactly 1 per confirmed 5-minute bar, in the **outer, stateful** context —
  never inside a security expression, for the same reason as everything else in §1.
- For Case A, may mirror chart-native `bar_index` directly or route through the same
  detection path as B/C (§5) — no correctness difference.

### 7.2 Confirmation / no duplicate processing / no skipped bars

- **Case A:** trivial — one chart-native confirmed bar is one 5m bar.
- **Case B:** `request.security_lower_tf()` returns, per chart-bar execution, only the
  intrabars newly available within that chart bar's own dilation; it does not re-return
  already-processed intrabars from a prior chart bar. This is documented behavior.
  **[OPEN VERIFICATION REQUIRED]** whether the *last* element of the returned array, on a
  still-**unconfirmed** chart bar, is guaranteed already-closed or can be the still-forming
  intrabar. Community documentation consistently describes the function as surfacing
  completed intrabars, but this specific edge was not confirmed against the reference
  manual in this pass. **This is the single highest-priority item to verify before
  implementation** — processing an incomplete 5m bar as final would be a correctness
  failure, not a cosmetic one.
- **Case C:** the §4 timestamp-change detection, gated on the outer bar's
  `barstate.isconfirmed`, fires at most once per genuinely new 5m close by construction —
  the fetched value only changes when the underlying 5m bar actually closes.

### 7.3 No reprocessing on chart redraw

Pine's realtime/historical redraw behavior must not cause a given confirmed 5-minute bar to
run through step [4] (§2) more than once over the life of the chart. This is the same
replay-safety property M9/M10 already guarantee for their own registries
(`barstate.isconfirmed`-gated, stable index-order iteration); the Data Layer must provide an
equivalent guarantee one level up — for *whether the pipeline runs at all* on a given script
execution.

