## §13. Formal Acceptance Test

### 13.1 Primary acceptance test

Once implemented (not before), correctness is established by comparative replay across
three charts, same historical period:

```
5m chart   → record M10 signals
15m chart  → record M10 signals
30m chart  → record M10 signals
```

For every recommendation produced, the following must match exactly across all three chart
display timeframes:

| Field | Must match |
|---|---|
| 5m execution timestamp | ✓ |
| Action (BUY/SELL) | ✓ |
| Entry | ✓ |
| Stop Loss | ✓ |
| Take Profit | ✓ |
| RR | ✓ |
| Confidence | ✓ |
| Rating | ✓ |
| Exit reason (when resolved) | ✓ |

A pass is the acceptance criterion for the Data Layer implementation. Any mismatch indicates
an intrabar-ordering defect (§10) or a non-repainting violation (§9), not a fault in M10 or
M11.1, which remain frozen throughout.

### 13.2 Implementation-level tests

1. **Alignment test (Case C, 3m specifically):** empirically confirm the detection-lag bound
   described in §4 — the 5m bar is eventually detected correctly and exactly once, even if
   not immediately at its true close, and no 5m bar is ever silently skipped.
2. **Native ATR parity (Case A):** on a 5-minute chart, confirm the §6 buffer-based ATR
   reimplementation produces results identical to Pine's native `ta.atr(14)` on the same
   bars.
3. **Native pivot parity (Case A):** on a 5-minute chart, confirm the §6 buffer-based
   pivot-high/pivot-low reimplementation produces results identical to Pine's native
   `ta.pivothigh`/`ta.pivotlow` on the same bars.
4. **FVG/OB historical-buffer parity:** confirm the §6 ring-buffer-based `close[1]`/
   `open[1]`/`high[2]`/`low[2]` reads match what native Pine offsets would have produced on
   an equivalent 5-minute-native series.
5. **Lower-TF intrabar completeness (Case B):** resolve the §7.2 open verification item —
   confirm directly whether the last element of a `request.security_lower_tf()` array on an
   unconfirmed chart bar is guaranteed already-closed, and add an explicit guard if not.
6. **No duplicate 5m processing:** across all three cases, confirm no confirmed 5-minute bar
   is ever run through step [4] more than once.
7. **No skipped 5m bars:** across all three cases, confirm every confirmed 5-minute bar is
   run through step [4] exactly once — none silently dropped.
8. **Expiry timing:** confirm `POOL_EXPIRY_BARS = 200`, `POI_EXPIRY_BARS = 150`,
   `M9_CANDIDATE_EXPIRY_BARS = 150`, and `M10_REC_EXPIRY_BARS = 100` now expire at the same
   fixed wall-clock offset (e.g., 500 minutes for the 100-bar constant) regardless of chart
   display timeframe.
9. **Realtime/historical consistency:** confirm identical M10 output whether a given segment
   of history is evaluated in realtime, tick-by-tick, or in a single historical replay pass.
10. **No state mutation inside security expressions (compile-time check):** confirm the
    implementation never attempts to call mutation-bearing M5–M10 logic from inside any
    `request.security*()` expression. Per §1, this should fail to compile if violated, but
    remains an explicit code-review item given how central the constraint is to the whole
    design.
11. **Performance/resource testing:** confirm `request.security*()` call count stays within
    the 40-call-per-script ceiling (§14), and that per-bar computation cost on high chart
    timeframes (up to six or more M5→M10 passes per chart bar) remains within acceptable
    bounds for long historical replays.

