## §14. Performance / Resource Risks (carried forward from v1.0 §12, confirmed by feasibility report)

- **`request.security()` 40-call-per-script ceiling.** The script already uses 3 calls for
  the MTF bias engine (`P9_HTF_1/2/3`). The Data Layer adds at most a small, fixed number
  more — one scalar 5m fetch for Case C, or a handful of `request.security_lower_tf()` calls
  for Case B — comfortably within budget, but to be counted explicitly once implemented
  rather than assumed safe.
- **Intrabar retrieval caps** (100K–200K bars depending on plan tier) and `calc_bars_count`
  bound how much 5m intrabar history is available per `request.security_lower_tf()` call —
  a real ceiling on long historical replay at high chart timeframes (e.g., Daily, Weekly).
  **[OPEN VERIFICATION REQUIRED]** exact behavior/degradation once this ceiling is hit is
  not resolved by this document; flagged for the implementation phase.
- **Per-chart-bar computation multiplication.** Running the (partially reimplemented)
  M5→M10 stack up to six or more times per chart bar multiplies per-bar computation cost
  proportionally — a real, architecturally unavoidable performance question given the
  intrabar-sequencing requirement (§10), to be empirically tested once implemented.
- **Manual buffer maintenance overhead** (ring buffers, running ATR state) is lightweight
  relative to the pipeline logic itself and not expected to be a material performance
  concern.
- **Registry/event/audit volume increases** on chart timeframes above 5m, since a single
  chart-bar execution can now produce multiple registry pushes (up to six, in the 30m case)
  instead of one. This does not change registry architecture (**[FROZEN]**) but does change
  fill rate under high chart timeframes — worth stress-testing against
  `MAX_TRADE_REC_REGISTRY`, `MAX_M9_REGISTRY`, etc. before declaring the layer complete.

