## Issue 2 — The "Unconfirmed Last Intrabar" Problem: RESOLVED BY ARCHITECTURAL RULE

**New rule, replacing v1.1 §7.2's Case B handling:**

> For chart timeframes above 5 minutes, the lower-timeframe intrabar arrays are retrieved
> and processed **only when the outer chart bar is itself confirmed** — never while it is
> still forming.

```
30m chart bar forming
        │
        ▼
   DO NOT RUN M5→M10
        │
   30m chart bar closes
        │
        ▼
   barstate.isconfirmed = true
        │
        ▼
   retrieve/process all six contained 5m bars, chronologically
        │
   5m #1 → M5→M10
   5m #2 → M5→M10
   ...
   5m #6 → M5→M10
```

**Why this resolves the open item rather than just working around it:** v1.1's concern was
whether the *last element* of a `request.security_lower_tf()` array, fetched on a
**still-forming** outer bar, could itself still be an incomplete intrabar. Under this rule,
the array is never fetched or acted upon while the outer bar is unconfirmed at all — the
question "is the last element closed?" becomes moot, because by the time the fetch happens
(on the confirmed 30m bar), the entire 30-minute window — all six 5m bars, including what
would have been the "last element" — is definitionally in the past. There is no longer a
still-forming candidate to worry about.

`barstate.isconfirmed` is documented as true on historical bars and on the closing update of
a realtime bar, and is documented as **not** usable inside `request.security()`. That
constraint is respected here: the confirmation check gates the outer script's decision to
call `request.security_lower_tf()` at all — it does not appear inside the security
expression itself, consistent with v1.1 §1's side-effect boundary.

**This item is removed from [OPEN VERIFICATION REQUIRED]** and replaced with the rule above.

**What remains, and is explicitly kept per your instruction:** TradingView's own
documentation notes that realtime lower-timeframe data can differ from historical data
because of feed/data-provider behavior (tick aggregation differences between live and
replayed history). This is a **separate, broader caveat** from the one just resolved — it is
about data-provider fidelity, not about Pine's confirmation semantics — and it remains in
the non-repainting discussion (§7 below) as a documented, irreducible caveat rather than an
open verification item, since it is not something any code-side rule can fully eliminate.

