## §5. Corrected Scope Statement — Superseding v1.1 §8's "Narrow Refactor" Claim

v1.1 §8 stated the refactor burden was "concentrated almost entirely in
`f_m5_update_structure` (pivots, ATR) and the two M7 pattern-detection functions." **This
undersold the actual scope, and the source audit above corrects it.**

The distinction v1.1 drew — between operations needing genuine *reimplementation*
(pivots/ATR/historical offsets) and operations needing only *parameter substitution*
(current-bar OHLC, `bar_index`) — is **still the right distinction to draw**, and it still
holds up against the source. What v1.1 did not make explicit is that **"substitution" is
itself a code change to nearly every function in M5–M10**, because **none of these
functions currently accept OHLC, `bar_index`, or confirmation state as parameters at all —
they all read bare Pine identifiers directly.** There is no existing parameter to swap a
value into; a parameter has to be added to the function signature and threaded through
every call site, including the six-line call chain at the bottom of the script
(`f_m5_update_structure(S)`, `f_m6_update_liquidity(S)`, ... `f_m10_coordinator(S)`), which
itself will need to become "call this chain once per confirmed 5-minute bar, passing that
bar's data," rather than "call this chain once per confirmed chart bar."

**This does not change what needs to be preserved** — no formula, threshold, registry, dedup
rule, or Stage-ordering changes anywhere in M5–M10, confirmed function-by-function in §4.
It changes how large a *mechanical* undertaking the "substitution-only" work is: it is not a
handful of call sites, it is essentially every trading-logic function in the pipeline,
because bare-series reads are the pipeline's *only* current mechanism for accessing market
data.

