## 11. Performance / Resource Risks

- **`request.security()` 40-call-per-script ceiling.** The script currently uses 3 calls for
  the MTF bias engine (`P9_HTF_1/2/3` inside `f_get_htf_snapshot`, called three times from
  `f_m7_update_mtf`). The Data Layer adds at most a small, fixed number more — one scalar 5m
  fetch for Case C, or a handful of `request.security_lower_tf()` calls for Case B —
  comfortably within budget, but should be counted explicitly once implemented.
- **Intrabar retrieval caps** (100K–200K bars, plan-tier dependent) and `calc_bars_count`
  bound how much 5m intrabar history is available per `request.security_lower_tf()` call — a
  real ceiling on long historical replay at high chart timeframes (e.g., Daily, Weekly).
- **Per-chart-bar computation multiplication.** Running the (partially reimplemented)
  M5→M10 stack up to six or more times per chart bar (30m case) multiplies per-bar
  computation cost proportionally — an unavoidable consequence of the intrabar-sequencing
  requirement, not a design flaw, and should be empirically tested once implemented.
- **Manual buffer maintenance overhead** (ring buffers, running ATR state) is lightweight
  relative to the pipeline logic itself.
- **Registry/event/audit volume increases** on chart timeframes above 5m, since a single
  chart-bar execution can now produce multiple registry pushes (up to six, in the 30m case)
  instead of one — worth stress-testing against `MAX_TRADE_REC_REGISTRY`, `MAX_M9_REGISTRY`,
  `MAX_POI_REGISTRY`, etc. before declaring the layer complete.

