## Is Design B Technically Possible in Pine v6?

**As literally specified — "every closed 5m bar processed once, immediately, with registry
state persisting correctly across subsequent ticks of the still-forming outer bar" — no.**
This is not an implementation-difficulty finding; it is a direct consequence of documented
`var` rollback semantics applied to a script built entirely on `var`-declared registries. Any
implementation attempting this literally will either lose data (silently, on the very next
tick) or duplicate it (if it tries to compensate by reprocessing).

**A variant is technically possible, with caveats:** re-deriving the full state for all of
an outer bar's currently-closed intrabars, from scratch, on every realtime tick, using
ordinary `var` registries that simply get rebuilt identically each time. This satisfies "no
duplicate processing" and "no data loss" *if and only if* the replay logic is made fully
idempotent — which the current M5–M10 source is not designed for (per the prior report's
function-by-function audit: registry mutation throughout M5–M10 is push-based, not
upsert-based). Even if made idempotent, this variant:

- does not commit anything durably until the outer bar's own closing tick, so it does not
  actually deliver the "acted upon 5 minutes after the 5m bar closes, reliably" benefit that
  motivates Design B in the first place — it only makes the *display* update faster, not the
  underlying commitment;
- multiplies per-tick computation cost by the number of currently-closed intrabars in the
  forming outer bar (up to six, near the 30-minute bar's close), on **every tick**, not just
  once per 5-minute boundary — a materially larger resource cost than Design A's "compute
  once, at confirmation" pattern, and one that scales with tick frequency, which is
  unpredictable and outside the script's control;
- still inherits the documented realtime/historical intrabar data-feed discrepancy caveat,
  meaning even a technically-correct idempotent replay is not guaranteed to match what
  eventually commits at the outer bar's true close.

`varip` is technically capable of solving the narrow "does the mutation survive to the next
tick" problem, but disqualified for this architecture specifically because it makes
historical replay unable to reproduce the realtime behavior it would be used to implement —
which directly undermines the project's own acceptance-testing methodology, not just as a
style concern but as a correctness one.

**Recommendation carried into Architecture v1.3: retain Design A (outer-bar confirmation),
as already adopted in Architecture v1.2's resolution of the prior "unconfirmed last
intrabar" question.** The latency Design B would buy is real but is not obtainable without
either breaking the registries' `var`-based persistence model or accepting a persistent,
turned-out-to-be-illusory-in-practice benefit from a full-replay variant that costs
materially more compute for a result that still is not final until the outer bar closes.

