## 1. Question 1 — Can `request.security_lower_tf()` return data while the outer bar is
## still forming?

**Yes, confirmed.** This is not merely permitted, it is the function's primary documented
use case. Per TradingView's own release documentation:

> `request.security_lower_tf()` ... makes it easier to request data from a lower timeframe
> than the chart's ... returning an array containing one value of the supplied expression
> for each intrabar. Note that **the number of intrabars may vary for each chart bar.**

The array grows as more intrabars complete during the life of a chart bar — including a
still-forming, unconfirmed one. At 09:40, mid-formation of a 09:30–10:00 outer bar,
`request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, "5", [open, high, low, close, time])` would be
expected to return an array containing the 09:30 and 09:35 intrabars (the two that have
closed so far), and that array would grow to include 09:40's intrabar once it closes, and so
on, up to all six by 10:00.

**So Question 1's answer is unambiguously yes.** The data is available early. This is not
where the problem is.

---

## 2. Question 2 — Do stateful mutations made from that early data survive?

**No — not without `varip`, and `varip` is disqualified for the registries themselves (§4).
This is the actual finding, and it is the one that matters.**

Pine's execution model, documented on the `varip` reference page (which explicitly links to
and summarizes the execution-model page), states the mechanism plainly:

> Whereas scripts only execute once at the close of historical bars, when a script is
> running in realtime, it executes every time the chart's feed detects a price or volume
> update. **At every realtime update, Pine's runtime normally resets the values of a
> script's variables to their last committed value, i.e., the value they held when the
> previous bar closed.** This is generally handy, as each realtime script execution starts
> from a known state...

This is the **rollback process** the `varip` keyword exists specifically to let a variable
*escape*. Read literally and applied to your example:

- At 09:40, a new tick arrives. The script executes. `request.security_lower_tf()` returns
  an array now containing the 09:35 intrabar. The script detects this, runs
  `M5→M6→M7→M8→M9→M10` against it, and mutates `var`-scoped registries: pushes to
  `g_registry`, `g_poi_registry`, `g_m9_registry`, `g_trade_rec_registry`, increments
  `S.counters.next_pool_id`/`next_poi_id`/`next_trade_rec_id`, pushes to
  `g_poi_dedup_keys`/`g_m9_dedup_keys`/`g_m10_dedup_keys`, etc.
- At 09:45, a **new** tick arrives (still the same, still-unconfirmed 09:30–10:00 outer
  bar). Per the quoted mechanism, the Pine runtime resets every `var`-scoped variable —
  every registry array, every counter, every dedup-key list — back to **the value it held
  when the previous bar closed**, i.e., back to its state as of 09:30, before the 09:40
  tick's mutation ever happened. The 09:40 tick's registry push is gone. Not "superseded" —
  gone, as if it never occurred.
- The script then re-executes from that rolled-back baseline. `request.security_lower_tf()`
  now returns an array containing both 09:35 and 09:40's intrabars (09:40 having closed by
  09:45). If the script's logic is "detect newly-available intrabars and process each
  exactly once," it would need some way to know "09:35 was already processed" — but the
  `var`-scoped dedup-key list that would have recorded that fact was itself just rolled
  back. Without an escape from rollback, the script cannot distinguish "already processed
  this forming bar" from "never processed," because the only place that fact could have
  been recorded is exactly the kind of state that just got wiped.

**This is not a corner case or an unlikely race condition — it is the normal, every-tick
behavior of a forming realtime bar, by design**, and it happens on every single realtime
update until the bar finally closes.

---

## 3. What Actually Gets Committed, and Why It Converges to Design A

Given the mechanism in §2, trace through what happens across the *whole* life of the
09:30–10:00 bar, not just one tick:

- Every tick, from 09:31 through 09:59, executes from the **same rolled-back baseline**
  (state as of 09:30) and reprocesses **all currently-available closed intrabars** from
  that baseline, in order, from scratch.
- The tick that finally **closes** the bar at 10:00 (the one where `barstate.isconfirmed`
  becomes true) is the **only** tick whose result is actually committed forward into the
  next bar's baseline.
- At that final tick, `request.security_lower_tf()` returns all six completed 5m intrabars,
  and — because every tick replays from the same 09:30 baseline — this final tick's replay
  of "all six intrabars, in order, from the 09:30 state" is **exactly** what Design A
  already specifies: wait for outer-bar confirmation, then process every contained intrabar
  chronologically, once, from the pre-bar state.

**So the durable, replay-consistent outcome of "try to process intrabars early" and "wait
for confirmation" is identical** — because Pine's rollback mechanism forces every
intermediate attempt at early processing to be thrown away and effectively re-derives
Design A's behavior at the moment of confirmation regardless. The only difference between
"trying to process early" and "just waiting" is that the early attempts do real,
non-trivial computational work (six-module pipeline runs, registry pushes, event/audit
emissions) that gets discarded on every single tick before the bar closes — a pure
performance cost with no durable benefit, and, if not handled with extreme care, a source of
transient duplicate-looking state that could confuse anything reading the registries
mid-formation (see §5).

---

## 4. Why `varip` Does Not Solve This for the Registries

`varip` variables are explicitly designed to escape the rollback process described in §2 —
their value **does** persist across ticks within the same forming bar. This is real and
documented. The question is whether it is usable here, and it is not, for a
documented, disqualifying reason:

> Because `varip` only affects the behavior of your code in the realtime bar, ... **backtest
> results on strategies built using logic based on `varip` will be meaningless, as `varip`
> behavior cannot be simulated on historical bars.** This also entails that plots on
> historical bars will not be able to reproduce the script's behavior in realtime.

This is a direct, hard conflict with the Data Layer's core requirement, carried through
every version of this architecture: identical results across 5m/15m/30m charts, and
identical results in historical replay versus realtime. If `g_trade_rec_registry`,
`g_poi_registry`, `g_m9_registry`, or any of the trading-logic counters/dedup arrays were
declared `varip` to make early-intrabar mutations survive ticks, the script's behavior in
**historical replay** (where `varip`'s special rollback-escaping behavior does not apply the
same way, since historical bars execute once and don't have the same tick-by-tick realtime
progression) would diverge from its behavior **live**. That is precisely the discrepancy
the entire Data Layer exists to eliminate. `varip` cannot be used on the trading registries
or on any counter/dedup state that feeds them, without reintroducing the exact problem this
architecture is meant to solve — this is a documented incompatibility, not a design
preference.

**Where `varip` remains legitimately usable:** for genuinely realtime-only, non-durable
bookkeeping that has no historical-replay equivalent to be consistent with — for example, a
"have I already fired an alert for this specific 5m bar's close" flag used purely to
prevent duplicate `alert()` calls during a forming bar (§6). This is exactly the use case
`varip` was introduced for (the official `varip` example script tracks realtime update
counts and volume-direction timing, not durable trading state), and it does not touch the
registries or affect what gets committed when the bar closes.

**Open item, syntactic rather than architectural:** whether `varip` can be declared on
array-of-UDT types (`array<TradeRecommendationInstance>`, etc.) the way `var` currently is,
was not confirmed in this pass either way. **[OPEN VERIFICATION REQUIRED]** — moot for this
report's conclusion, since `varip` is disqualified on historical-parity grounds regardless
of whether it is syntactically legal on those types.

