## 5. Design A vs. Design B — Direct Comparison

| Criterion | Design A (outer-bar confirmation) | Design B (confirmed-intrabar processing) |
|---|---|---|
| **Historical determinism** | Full — historical bars execute once each; "process all contained intrabars at confirmation" is the *only* thing that happens, with no rollback ambiguity to reason about. | Not achievable in the sense specified. Per §2–§3, any attempt at "process each intrabar as it closes" durably reduces to Design A's behavior anyway, once you account for rollback — so Design B does not describe a genuinely different historical execution path, only a different (and non-functional) intended realtime one. |
| **Realtime latency** | Recommendation is not durably committed until the outer bar closes (e.g., up to ~30 minutes of latency on a 30m chart for the last-contained intrabar). | *Intended* latency is near-immediate (minutes, matching the 5m bar's own close) — but per §2–§3, this intent cannot be realized durably. A live-*display* version of low latency is achievable (§6), but not a live-*commit* version. |
| **Repaint risk** | None — nothing is shown or committed as a signal until it is final. | If misimplemented (durable registry mutation attempted mid-formation, without full awareness of rollback), this is a direct repaint-risk vector: a value seen live could vanish or be silently replaced on a later tick, which is functionally a repaint even if `lookahead_off` is honored everywhere. |
| **Duplicate risk** | Structurally zero — the confirmation gate means the six-intrabar replay happens exactly once, at exactly one point (bar close). | High, if attempted naively without the rollback mechanism in mind (§2's walkthrough) — apparent "duplicate" pushes would actually occur every tick, though see §3: because none of these mid-formation pushes survive rollback, they would not accumulate into genuinely duplicated *committed* registry entries, only wasted computation and (if not carefully isolated from any always-on side effect such as `alert()`) potential duplicate *notifications*. |
| **State persistence** | Registries, counters, and dedup arrays behave exactly as `var` is documented to behave — no special handling needed. | Requires either `varip` (disqualified, §4) or an elaborate, still-incomplete workaround; no clean mechanism exists. |
| **5m/15m/30m parity** | Preserved — the committed state after any given historical period is provably the same regardless of which tick sequence produced it, since only the confirming tick's replay ever commits. | Cannot be evaluated independently of Design A's guarantee, because — per §3 — Design B's durable behavior collapses into Design A's at the moment of commitment. There is no genuinely distinct "Design B" outcome to compare for parity purposes. |
| **Suitability for live trading** | High for durable signals: what is committed is trustworthy and will not be revised. Latency up to one outer-bar length is the tradeoff, exactly as any HTF-confirmation-gated system accepts. | Not viable as specified for durable signals, for the reasons above. Viable only as a *supplementary, clearly-labeled live preview* layered on top of Design A (§6) — not as a replacement execution model. |

**Conclusion of the comparison:** Design B, as posed in your question — durable, registry-
committing processing of a 5m intrabar the moment it closes, without waiting for the outer
bar — is not something Pine's execution model supports, for a specific, documented reason
(§2), not a matter of it being merely harder to implement correctly. Design A remains the
correct architectural rule.

