## §6. Case B and Case C — Data Acquisition Mechanisms

### Case B (chart > 5m: 15m, 30m, 1H+)

`request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, "5", [open, high, low, close, time])` is
confirmed appropriate. A tuple expression passed to this function returns a **tuple of
synchronized arrays**, index-aligned and ordered chronologically by intrabar time — this is
confirmed against current Pine v6 documentation and is not an open item. Retrieval and
processing of this data is gated entirely by the §3 outer-bar-confirmation rule.

**Confirmed platform limits (not open questions):** intrabar retrieval is capped at
100K–200K bars depending on plan tier (100K for Basic/Essential/Plus/Premium, 125K for
Expert, 200K for Ultimate), and `calc_bars_count` governs how much intrabar history a given
call retrieves — a real constraint on long historical replay at high chart timeframes (§14).

### Case C (chart < 5m: 1m, 3m)

`request.security_lower_tf()` is invalid here by definition — it requires a target
timeframe strictly lower than the chart's own, and "5" is not lower than "1" or "3." The
mechanism is ordinary `request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "5", [open, high, low, close,
time], lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off, gaps=barmerge.gaps_off)` — the standard "request a
higher timeframe from a lower-timeframe chart" pattern, non-repainting by default.

**Detection of a newly completed 5m bar, exactly once:** compare the fetched 5m bar's `time`
against a `var`-stored previous value, evaluated only when the outer (1m/3m) bar is
`barstate.isconfirmed`. A change in the fetched timestamp means exactly one new 5-minute bar
has completed, and processing runs exactly once. This mirrors the non-repainting discipline
the existing MTF bias engine (`f_get_htf_snapshot`) already relies on.

**1m and 3m are not equivalent — preserved distinction, not weakened:**

- **1m:** bar boundaries divide evenly into 5-minute boundaries. Detection is exact and
  immediate.
- **3m:** bar boundaries (`:00, :03, :06, :09, :12...`) do **not** evenly divide 5-minute
  boundaries (`:00, :05, :10, :15...`) — no common factor below 15 minutes. Detection of a
  closed 5m bar on a 3m chart can lag its true close by up to several minutes. The **data**,
  once detected, is correct; only the **timing** of detection is chart-bar-boundary
  dependent. This is documented as a bounded, known limitation specific to any chart
  timeframe that does not evenly divide 5 minutes — it is not claimed to be identical to the
  1m case anywhere in this document.

