## §14. Performance / Resource Risks (preserved, not re-estimated)

- **`request.security()` 40-call-per-script ceiling.** The script currently uses 3 calls
  (the MTF bias engine, `f_get_htf_snapshot` called three times from `f_m7_update_mtf`). The
  Data Layer adds a small, fixed number more — one scalar fetch for Case C, or a handful of
  `request.security_lower_tf()` calls for Case B.
- **Intrabar retrieval caps** (100K non-professional plans, 125K Expert, 200K Ultimate) and
  `calc_bars_count` bound available 5m intrabar history per call — a real ceiling on long
  historical replay at high chart timeframes.
- **Per-chart-bar computation multiplication.** Running the M5→M10 stack up to six or more
  times per chart bar (30m case) multiplies per-bar computation cost proportionally — an
  unavoidable consequence of the intrabar-sequencing requirement (§3), not a design flaw.
- **Manual buffer maintenance overhead** (ring buffers, running ATR state) is lightweight
  relative to the pipeline logic itself.
- **Registry/event/audit volume increases** on chart timeframes above 5m, since a single
  chart-bar execution can now produce multiple registry pushes (up to six, in the 30m case)
  instead of one — worth stress-testing against `MAX_TRADE_REC_REGISTRY`, `MAX_M9_REGISTRY`,
  `MAX_POI_REGISTRY`, etc. before declaring the layer complete.

No performance measurements are invented here; all of the above are documented, structural
constraints or direct, unavoidable consequences of the architecture's own requirements.

