## §4. Data Availability vs. Durable Commitment — unchanged from v1.3

Confirmed correct and unchanged by review — quoted directly: *"§4 correctly distinguishes
availability from commitment — keep that... This is one of the strongest parts of v1.3."*

**Data availability — YES.** `request.security_lower_tf()` returns a growing array of
intrabars, ordered by intrabar timestamp, as they complete — including while the outer chart
bar is still forming.

**Durable M5→M10 registry commitment — NO, under the current `var`-based architecture.**
Pine's documented execution model rolls back `var`-scoped state to its last-committed value
on every realtime tick of a still-forming bar; only the tick that closes the bar is ever
actually committed. No mutation made before outer-bar confirmation survives.

### §4.1 `varip` — Correction: Precision of the Prohibition, Not Its Substance

**Correction applied.** v1.3 stated the `varip` prohibition in a way that could be read as
"`varip` is universally unacceptable." That overstates the finding. The corrected framing:

**`varip` itself is not forbidden as a Pine language feature — it does genuinely escape the
rollback process, exactly as documented, and it has legitimate uses.** What is prohibited,
specifically and only, is:

> **No `varip` for authoritative trading state — registries, lifecycle state, counters, or
> deduplication state — that affects committed M5–M10 results.**

The reason remains as previously stated: `varip`'s own documentation confirms it persists
across realtime executions, unlike ordinary `var`-scoped variables subject to rollback, but
also explicitly warns that realtime behavior relying on `varip` cannot be reproduced on
historical bars in the same way. For any variable whose value must correspond between
historical replay and live execution — which is every trading registry, counter, and dedup
array in M5–M10 — that non-reproducibility is disqualifying. It is not disqualifying for
variables with no historical-parity requirement, which is exactly why the §15 future-preview
exception (a `varip`-based alert-dedup flag with no trading-registry equivalent) remains
consistent with this section, unchanged.

### §4.2 Documented Feed/Provider Caveat — unchanged from v1.3

Realtime lower-timeframe data can differ from historical replay due to feed/data-provider
tick-aggregation behavior. This is not a Pine repaint mechanism — it is a property of the
underlying data feed. Retained as a documented, irreducible limitation to disclose (see also
§12's corrected wording, which keeps this distinct from the architecture's own determinism
guarantee).

