## §6. Case B and Case C — Corrected: Edge-Case Contracts Strengthened

### Case B (chart > 5m: 15m, 30m, 1H+)

`request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, "5", [open, high, low, close, time])` remains
appropriate. **Confirmed, per current documentation: this tuple expression counts as a
single unique `request.*()` call, not five** — see §14's corrected resource accounting.
Retrieval and processing remain gated entirely by the §3 outer-bar-confirmation rule.

**New — Correction 9: Partial intrabar availability, explicit validation rule.**

v1.3 implicitly assumed that outer-bar confirmation on a 30-minute chart always yields
exactly six 5-minute intrabars. Current documentation states the number of intrabars
returned "can vary" according to available data and provider coverage, and retrieval is
additionally bounded by the plan-dependent intrabar limit (§14). This architecture therefore
adds an explicit validation rule, owned by the Data Layer, not by M5–M10:

> **At outer-bar confirmation, the Data Layer must verify that the retrieved intrabar set
> covers the expected 5-minute intervals for that chart bar** (e.g., six intervals for a
> 30-minute bar). If fewer intrabars are returned than expected, the Data Layer must
> distinguish between, and handle separately:
>
> 1. **Legitimate absence of market/data bars** (e.g., a session gap, a low-liquidity
>    period with no ticks) — process only the intervals that genuinely exist; this is not an
>    error.
> 2. **Provider/data-feed limitation** (per §4.2's documented caveat) — flag for audit/
>    diagnostic visibility; do not silently proceed as though nothing were missing.
> 3. **Request-history limitation** (the plan-dependent intrabar/`calc_bars_count` cap, §14)
>    — flag as a known, bounded limitation of the deployment's plan tier.
> 4. **Actual implementation failure** (a bug in the fetch/detection logic) — this is the
>    one case that must be treated as a defect, not a data characteristic.
>
> **The Data Layer must never fabricate a missing 5-minute bar to fill a gap.** Doing so
> would corrupt the buffer (§9) and misrepresent genuine price history to M5–M10.

This rule does not change Design A (§3) or the confirmation/dedup contract (§11); it adds an
explicit, named validation step at the point where the intrabar array is retrieved, so that
"fewer bars than expected" is a handled, classified condition rather than a silent assumption
of completeness.

### Case C (chart < 5m: 1m, 3m)

`request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "5", [open, high, low, close, time],
lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off, gaps=barmerge.gaps_off)` remains the mechanism, gated on
the outer bar's `barstate.isconfirmed`.

**Correction 6 — the detection invariant is restated, less absolutely.**

v1.3 stated: *"A change in the fetched timestamp means exactly one new 5-minute bar has
completed."* This assumes a strict one-to-one correspondence between "outer bar confirms"
and "exactly one new 5-minute boundary crossed," which is not guaranteed if chart data is
missing or irregular (e.g., a gap in the 1m/3m feed spanning more than one 5-minute
interval). The corrected invariant:

> A timestamp change identifies a **newly observed** 5-minute bar. The implementation must
> **verify** — not assume — that no more than one previously unprocessed 5-minute boundary
> has been crossed between confirmed outer-bar executions. If the observed timestamp has
> advanced by more than one 5-minute interval since the last processed bar (detectable by
> comparing the gap between the newly observed timestamp and the last-processed timestamp
> against the 5-minute interval length), the implementation must explicitly handle the
> multiple newly-available 5-minute bars — processing each, chronologically, per the same
> "exactly once, no skips" contract as Case B — rather than silently processing only the
> most recent one and discarding the intermediate bar(s).

This is retained as an **implementation verification point** (§16), not silently assumed
away: the architecture specifies the required behavior (detect and handle multi-bar jumps
explicitly) without asserting, ungrounded, that such jumps cannot occur.

**Correction 7 — a newly observed timestamp is not, by itself, proof of confirmation.**

v1.3 did not make this explicit. Corrected statement, now explicit in this architecture:

> A newly observed 5-minute timestamp from `request.security()` is not by itself proof that
> the corresponding 5-minute bar is confirmed/closed — on a 3-minute chart specifically, the
> requested 5-minute bar can still be developing at the moment the 3-minute outer bar
> confirms. The implementation must only process **the previous** 5-minute bar upon
> detecting the **transition** to the next 5-minute timestamp, evaluated on a confirmed
> outer bar. It is the transition (the timestamp changing away from a given value) that
> proves the prior bar closed — not the mere presence of a timestamp value on any single
> evaluation.

This makes the non-repainting mechanism for Case C explicit rather than implicit, and is
consistent with, not a change to, the detection technique already described in v1.3.

**1m and 3m remain non-equivalent — unchanged from v1.3:**

- **1m:** bar boundaries divide evenly into 5-minute boundaries; detection is exact and
  immediate.
- **3m:** bar boundaries do not evenly divide 5-minute boundaries; detection can lag the
  true close by up to several minutes. The data, once detected, is correct; only detection
  timing is chart-bar-boundary dependent. Not claimed identical to the 1m case anywhere in
  this document.

