## §11. Confirmation / Deduplication Contract — CORRECTED (Correction 2)

**v1.3's absolute language is replaced with a scoped guarantee.**

v1.3 stated, in several places, "no skipped 5-minute bar, ever." This is stronger than
anything Pine — or the underlying data feed — can actually guarantee.
`request.security_lower_tf()` can return an empty or incomplete intrabar set when data is
unavailable (per §6's new partial-availability rule), and retrieval is bounded by the
plan-dependent intrabar limit (§14). Promising an absolute, unconditional "never skipped"
guarantee overstates what this architecture — or any Pine script — can deliver.

**Corrected guarantee, replacing every instance of the absolute wording:**

> **No skipped 5-minute bar within the accessible/requestable data returned by the Data
> Layer, subject to TradingView data availability, provider coverage, session structure,
> and request limits.**

This is a scoping correction, not a weakening of intent: the Data Layer still must process
every 5-minute bar it can access, exactly once, in order — but the guarantee is now honest
about what "can access" depends on, rather than implying a guarantee against platform-level
data gaps that no script-level architecture can close.

**Guaranteed, across all three cases, restated with the corrected scope:**

- Exactly one M5→M10 execution per confirmed 5-minute bar **that the Data Layer actually
  receives**.
- No duplicate execution of any 5-minute bar, ever — this part remains absolute, since it is
  a property of the confirmation-gating logic (§3, §6), not of data availability.
- No skipped 5-minute bar **within the data the Data Layer is able to retrieve** — per the
  corrected guarantee above.
- Strict chronological ordering when more than one 5-minute bar is processed within a single
  outer-bar confirmation (Case B), or when a multi-bar jump is detected (Case C, §6
  Correction 6).
- Persistent state updates originate only from the valid outer, confirmed execution context
  — never from an intermediate, unconfirmed tick (§4).

**Case A:** trivially satisfied — one confirmed chart bar is one confirmed 5m bar.

**Case B:** all *retrieved* contained 5m bars processed exactly once, chronologically,
triggered solely by outer-bar confirmation, with the §6 partial-availability validation rule
applied before processing begins.

**Case C:** the §6 timestamp-change detection, gated on outer-bar confirmation, with the
corrected multi-bar-jump handling (§6 Correction 6) and transition-proves-confirmation
invariant (§6 Correction 7) both explicit.

**Logical skipping vs. unavailable source data — new distinction, carried into §13's
acceptance tests:** this architecture explicitly distinguishes **logical skipping** (a bug
in the Data Layer's detection/processing logic, e.g., failing to process a 5-minute bar that
was genuinely available) from **unavailable source data** (a platform/feed limitation, per
§6's classification categories). Only the former is a defect against this architecture;
the latter is a disclosed, bounded characteristic of the underlying platform.

