## §12. Realtime / Historical Contract — CORRECTED (Correction 5)

**v1.3's "deterministic committed state" language is replaced with a two-part statement
that does not conflate architectural determinism with feed equivalence.**

v1.3 stated: *"The architecture guarantees deterministic committed state."* Read alone, this
risks being understood as a claim that TradingView's realtime and historical feeds will
always produce identical OHLC — which is not something this architecture, or any Pine
script, can guarantee, and which §4.2 itself already acknowledges as a documented
possibility of divergence. The corrected, two-part contract:

> **1. Deterministic execution given the same 5-minute input sequence.** Given identical
> 5-minute OHLC/time data, this architecture guarantees identical M5→M10 output — this is
> the property Design A (§3), the confirmation/dedup contract (§11), and the input-boundary
> refactor (§7–§8) are collectively designed to deliver, and it is the property the primary
> acceptance test (§13) validates.
>
> **2. Historical/realtime source-feed equivalence is a separate matter, subject to
> TradingView's documented feed/data-provider differences (§4.2), and is not guaranteed by
> this architecture.** If the underlying 5-minute data itself differs between a live feed
> and a subsequently retrieved historical replay — for reasons entirely outside this
> architecture's control — then output will differ accordingly, correctly, given point 1's
> guarantee that identical input produces identical output. This is a materially stronger
> and more honest claim than "no repainting," which this document no longer asserts as an
> unqualified property.

**Explicitly prohibited, under any circumstance, anywhere in this architecture —
unchanged from v1.3:**

- `varip`-declared **authoritative trading state** (registries, lifecycle state, counters,
  or deduplication state affecting committed M5–M10 results) — per §4.1's corrected, more
  precise scoping of the prohibition.
- Realtime-only trading state of any kind.
- Separate historical and realtime trading algorithms or code paths.
- Mid-formation durable registry mutation, on any chart timeframe.

**Non-repainting mechanism, restated with §6's corrections folded in:**

1. `lookahead_off` (default since Pine v3) prevents a security call from returning future
   information.
2. All pipeline-triggering decisions are gated on the **outer** chart bar's
   `barstate.isconfirmed`.
3. Case C's timestamp-**transition** detection (not mere timestamp presence — §6 Correction
   7) is immune to a still-updating realtime higher-timeframe value.
4. Case B's outer-bar-confirmation gate (§3) serves the same purpose at the intrabar level,
   now paired with the §6 partial-availability validation rule.
5. The §4.2 feed/provider caveat remains a documented, irreducible limitation, kept
   explicitly distinct from points 1–4 and from the point-1 determinism guarantee above.
6. The existing 60m/240m/D MTF bias mechanism already relies on this same discipline and is
   confirmed unaffected.

