## §6. Case B and Case C — Case B Unchanged; Case C Substantively Revised

### Case B (chart > 5m: 15m, 30m, 1H+) — unchanged from v1.4

`request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, "5", [open, high, low, close, time])`,
gated entirely by outer-bar confirmation (§3), with the partial-intrabar-availability
validation rule from v1.4 (four-way classification: legitimate absence / provider
limitation / request-history limitation / implementation failure; never fabricate a missing
bar) retained exactly, per review point 8: *"Keep that exactly."*

### Case C (chart < 5m: 1m, 3m) — **REVISED**

**v1.4's Case C design contained a genuine internal contradiction, not merely an imprecise
description, and is replaced below.**

**What was wrong:** v1.4 required Case C to "explicitly handle multiple newly-available
5-minute bars" when a multi-interval jump was detected, processing each intervening bar
chronologically. But the stated acquisition mechanism —
`request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "5", [open, high, low, close, time], ...)` — is a
**scalar, single-value HTF request**. Unlike `request.security_lower_tf()` (used in Case B),
which returns an **array** of every intrabar within the current chart bar,
`request.security()` requesting a *higher* timeframe than the chart returns only the
current (or, with an offset, one specific prior) value of that higher timeframe — never an
array of every intervening higher-timeframe bar. There is no mechanism available to Case C
that can retroactively supply the OHLC of 5-minute bars that closed **between** two
confirmed outer-bar executions, if more than one such bar closed in that gap. v1.4's
requirement to "process every intervening bar" was not satisfiable by the mechanism v1.4
itself specified for obtaining that data. This is corrected below by adopting the narrower,
implementable contract (Option A, per review), rather than by inventing a new acquisition
mechanism (Option B) that this document does not scope or authorize.

**A second, related problem, also corrected below:** v1.4 did not clearly separate two
distinct facts that a Case C implementation must establish independently:

- **Fact A — boundary:** the requested 5-minute HTF timestamp has advanced, meaning the
  prior 5-minute period has ended.
- **Fact B — data:** the OHLC values currently being read actually belong to that
  now-closed, confirmed 5-minute bar — not to the new, still-forming one.

v1.4's plain `request.security(..., lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off)` on the "current" HTF
expression does not, by itself, guarantee Fact B at the moment Fact A is detected. With
`lookahead_off` and no historical offset, the value returned for a still-forming higher-
timeframe bar continues to update as that bar develops in realtime — it is not fixed to a
confirmed snapshot the instant a boundary transition is observed. TradingView's documented,
recommended technique for guaranteed-confirmed HTF retrieval is different: **offset the
requested expression by one bar (`[1]`) and use `barmerge.lookahead_on`** — this combination
is what the reference documentation describes as returning "confirmed values on all bars,
regardless of bar state." Using the current-bar expression with `lookahead_off` alone
(v1.4's design, and also the existing `f_get_htf_snapshot`'s design, for context) is
adequate for informational bias display, which is what `f_get_htf_snapshot` uses it for
today, but is not, by itself, a rigorous guarantee suitable for supplying **authoritative**
5-minute OHLC to the entire M5–M10 pipeline — a materially higher-stakes use than a soft
MTF bias score.

### Corrected Case C Contract

```
1m / 3m chart
      ↓
detect 5m HTF boundary transition
   (compare a "current-value" timestamp poll against the last-processed timestamp,
    evaluated only on a confirmed outer bar — this establishes Fact A only)
      ↓
obtain the PREVIOUS confirmed 5m OHLC/time
   (via the offset-expression + lookahead_on technique — e.g., requesting
    [open[1], high[1], low[1], close[1], time[1]] with barmerge.lookahead_on
    from the "5" context — this establishes Fact B; boundary detection and
    confirmed-data retrieval are two separate steps, not one)
      ↓
validate that the retrieved bar is the next expected 5-minute execution bar
   (its timestamp is exactly one 5-minute interval after the last processed bar)
      ↓
process exactly once
      ↓
if the retrieved bar's timestamp is MORE than one 5-minute interval after the
last processed bar (i.e., more than one 5-minute boundary was crossed between
confirmed outer-bar executions):
      ↓
   do NOT fabricate or reconstruct the missing intervening bar(s)
      ↓
   classify the gap as unavailable/recovery-required data (per the same
   four-way classification discipline as Case B's partial-intrabar rule, §6
