zero-loss processing across chart-data gaps is ever required, that is new architecture — a
separate mechanism capable of independently retrieving each missing 5-minute bar's OHLC/time
(Option B, per review) — and is explicitly out of scope for this document, not silently
assumed to already be satisfied by the Case C mechanism as specified.

**"Transition proves confirmation" — restated with Fact A/Fact B separated, per review:**

> A detected transition to a new 5-minute HTF period establishes only that the preceding
> 5-minute period has ended (Fact A). The Data Layer must then separately obtain the
> preceding 5-minute bar's confirmed OHLC/time values through a non-repainting HTF retrieval
> mechanism (Fact B) before passing that bar downstream. The transition alone does not
> constitute possession of confirmed data — it constitutes only the knowledge that confirmed
> data now exists and should be retrieved through the mechanism above.

**1m and 3m remain non-equivalent — unchanged from v1.3/v1.4:**

- **1m:** bar boundaries divide evenly into 5-minute boundaries; detection is exact and
  immediate, and — per the corrected contract above — a multi-bar jump on a 1-minute chart
  would only occur due to an actual feed gap, not as a routine consequence of the chart/5m
  boundary relationship itself.
- **3m:** bar boundaries do not evenly divide 5-minute boundaries; detection can lag the true
  close by up to several minutes, and — now explicit — a 3-minute chart is inherently more
  exposed to the "more than one 5-minute boundary crossed" case than a 1-minute chart is,
  simply because of the longer gap between confirmed outer-bar executions, independent of
  any feed irregularity. This is a structural property of Case C on a 3-minute chart, not an
