stream* during a gap (classify, don't fabricate, resume) but did not specify what happens
to the *manually maintained buffer's* internal consistency when processing resumes. This is
a genuine correctness gap, not a documentation omission: the buffer's offset reads
(`close[1]`, `high[2]`), true-range calculation, and pivot windowed-extremum comparison are
all defined on the assumption that adjacent buffer entries correspond to temporally adjacent
5-minute bars. Resuming processing by simply appending the next successfully retrieved bar
next to the last pre-gap entry would silently violate that assumption — producing a spurious
true-range spike, a corrupted pivot window, and incorrect historical-offset reads, exactly
the kind of silent misrepresentation of price history §6's non-fabrication rule already
exists to prevent, occurring one layer lower, inside the buffer rather than in the event
stream.

